[38;5;231m⏺[39m [1mThe fork:[0m how the browser gets the validation code, given there's no build step today. This decides file layout and
  whether the new code is unit-testable at all — [38;5;153mapp.js[39m is currently a plain global script, while [38;5;153msrc/[39m uses CommonJS
  that the browser can't load.

  - [1mES modules[0m ([38;5;153m<script type="module">[39m, [38;5;153mexport[39m/[38;5;153mimport[39m) — buys real imports with no bundler, and the module is directly
    testable by Node. Costs: needs to be served over HTTP ([38;5;153mfile://[39m won't load modules), and it's a second module style
    alongside [38;5;153msrc/[39m's CommonJS unless you convert that too.
  - [1mGlobal script[0m ([38;5;153mvalidation.js[39m before [38;5;153mapp.js[39m, hangs a [38;5;153mValidation[39m object off [38;5;153mwindow[39m) — buys "open index.html and it
    works", zero setup. Costs: no import graph, awkward to unit test, and it's the pattern you'd migrate off later.
  - [1mES modules everywhere[0m — same as the first, plus converting [38;5;153msrc/index.js[39m and [38;5;153msrc/utils.js[39m off CommonJS so the repo
    has one style. Costs touching files unrelated to this task.

  [1mMy recommendation: ES modules, scoped to the web code only.[0m Testability is the main thing you're buying with a
  reusable layer, and [38;5;153mfile://[39m is a weak constraint if you're fine with [38;5;153mnpx serve[39m. I'd leave [38;5;153msrc/[39m alone — converting it
  is unrelated refactoring.
